  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nAn upper trough continued to dig over the western states which has\nhelped to bring rain showers to most of the area in the past 24\nhours. This looks to continue into tonight with the possibility of\nthunderstorms generating heavy rain, especially in the northeast\nzones later tonight.\n\nThe latest NBM is suggesting close to an inch in Roosevelt\ncounties just this evening but the HREF mean was showing this \npossibility also in other areas. There was the thought of issuing\na Flood Watch with the NBM output but the confidence is not high \nenough (GTE 50%) for a Watch. Therefore, will pass this on to \nlater shifts. However, being that antecednt conditions have been \nquite dry in the past month, flooding is highly unlikely. Impacts \naffected could be covered with an advisory.\n\nShowers are expected to end from west to east Friday with mainly\ndry conditions expected through the weekend. The upper trough will\nlinger off the West Coast with weaker southwest to west flow\nremaining over the area. This will allow for generally slight\nchances of showers/thunderstorms returning Sunday that lingers\ninto next week.\n\nLook for a slight warming trend to begin this weekend but will be\ntempered next week by disturbances that move in.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nLeft the base (NBM) forecast alone. Confidence has increased with\nrainfall amounts and temperatures for the next 2 to 3 days. TFJ",
